asr(5, 'conduction', 'Impulse conduction is slow in a thin unmyelinated fibre.', 'The depolarisation has to occur at every point along the membrane in sequence.', 0, 'The signal moves point by point along the whole length. In a myelinated fibre it jumps between nodes, which makes it much faster.');
asr(5, 'synapse', 'Electrical synapses can conduct impulses in both directions.', 'In an electrical synapse the neurotransmitter is released from the presynaptic neuron only.', 2, 'A is true as current flows through direct connections. R is false: electrical synapses do not use a neurotransmitter.');
stm(5, 'synapse', 'Impulse transmission across a chemical synapse involves the release of neurotransmitters from synaptic vesicles.', 'Neurotransmitter molecules enter the postsynaptic neuron and become part of its genetic material.', 2, 'I is right. II is wrong: the transmitter binds receptors on the postsynaptic membrane and does not enter the cell to become its genes.');
stm(5, 'neuron structure', 'The dorsal root ganglion has cell bodies of sensory neurons.', 'The ventral root of a spinal nerve carries efferent fibres.', 0, 'Both are correct. The dorsal root ganglion holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons, and the ventral root carries motor fibres out of the cord.');
